Give iOS a display to find.

backboardd dies on every boot and takes SpringBoard with it:

    Exception Type: EXC_CRASH (SIGABRT)
    Application Specific Information: abort() called
    ...
    11  Foundation      0x1a7981898
    12  backboardd      0x101002e3c
    13  backboardd      0x100feca9c

Those two frames are +0x1EE3C and +0x8A9C, and the first of them is this:

    adr x5, ... ; @"No window server display found"
    mov w4, #0x1a1
    bl  -[NSAssertionHandler handleFailureInFunction:file:lineNumber:description:]

-- backboarddaemon-195.65.1/megatrond/BKDisplay.m line 417, at the end of
BKDisplayStartWindowServer(), which does

    CADisplay *main = [CADisplay mainDisplay];
    id dispId       = [main displayId];
    id disp         = [server displayWithDisplayId:dispId];
    if (disp == nil) <assert>

SpringBoard is a bystander: FrontBoard throws because it cannot reach a
backboardd that is not there.

+[CADisplay mainDisplay] goes through IOMobileFramebuffer.framework, and the
framework wants very little. _IOMobileFramebufferGetDisplay reads:

    IOServiceGetMatchingServices(port, IOServiceMatching("IOMobileFramebuffer"), &it);
    while ((service = IOIteratorNext(it))) {
            if (service is AppleWirelessDisplay or AppleADBE1) continue;
            if (IOMobileFramebufferOpen(service, mach_task_self(), 0, &ref)) continue;
            if (IOMobileFramebufferIsMainDisplay(ref, &isMain)) continue;
            if (isMain == wanted) return ref;
    }

There is no property to match on and no score to beat: the first service of
that class which is not the wireless display and which answers is the main
display. IOMobileFramebufferOpen is IOServiceOpen with user client type 0,
and IsMainDisplay is a single call:

    IOConnectCallScalarMethod(connect, 18, NULL, 0, &out, &outCount);
    isMain = (out == 1);

So: a registered IOService of class IOMobileFramebuffer, a user client for
type 0, and selector 18 answering one scalar. That is the whole of it, and
that is the whole of this patch.

It is started from gIOKernelConfigTables rather than by a call from
anywhere, because that table is how a kernel with no kexts carries a
personality, and IOPlatformExpertDevice is the provider everything else in
it matches against too.

What this is not: a framebuffer. Nothing here touches the pixels -- those
belong to the shim's own console at 0x67000000 and still do. This is the
smallest thing that can fail differently, which is the point. Every other
selector returns kIOReturnUnsupported and logs its number, so whatever the
framework reaches for next is named on the next boot instead of guessed at
on this one.

It did not start the first time, and the reason is worth keeping.

The personality was on IOPlatformExpertDevice, which looked right -- it is
the provider every other entry in gIOKernelConfigTables matches against.
But IOKit starts one driver per nub, the highest scoring one, and that nub
already belongs to IOPlatformExpert at score 1000. IOPanicPlatform sits on
the same nub at score 0 for exactly that reason: it is what runs when
nothing else will. A third claimant is simply never tried, and the evidence
was that backboardd crash-looped without a single "unimplemented selector"
line -- it never found a service to open, so nothing in the user client was
ever reached.

IOResources is the nub that carries several drivers at once, which is what a
driver with no hardware of its own matches against, and a match category of
its own is how they stay out of each other's way there.

A line is logged on every successful open as well as at start, because
start() prints before launchd exists and has scrolled away long before
anything asks for a display.

The first boot with it running named the next six things to do.

backboardd found the service, opened the client, and asked for six selectors
that were not there:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: user client opened
    IOMobileFramebuffer: unimplemented selector 57 (in 0, out 1 scalars)
    IOMobileFramebuffer: unimplemented selector 7  (in 0, out 1 scalars)
    IOMobileFramebuffer: unimplemented selector 15 (in 2, out 1 scalars)
    IOMobileFramebuffer: unimplemented selector 22 (in 1, out 0 scalars)
    IOMobileFramebuffer: unimplemented selector 8  (in 0, out 2 scalars)

and then died of SIGSEGV instead of the SIGABRT it had been dying of, which
is the assertion gone and a null pointer in its place.

Each number was looked up by finding the IOConnectCallScalarMethod in
IOMobileFramebuffer.framework that passes it:

     7   kern_GetID              out 1
     8   kern_GetDisplaySize     out 2
    15   kern_SetDebugFlags      in 2, out 1
    22   kern_SetDisplayDevice   in 1
    57   kern_GetColorRemapMode  out 1

GetDisplaySize returns width then height: kern_GetDisplaySize loads the two
scalars together and runs ucvtf.2d over the pair, so what reaches the caller
is a CGSize of two doubles in that order. The numbers come from
PE_current_console() rather than from constants, because the shim already
told the kernel what the panel is and there is no sense in having a second
place for that to be wrong.

GetID returns 1. CADisplay keeps whatever it gets and hands it back as the
display it wants, so the value matters only in being non-zero and stable.
The rest are accepted and dropped.

And the boot after that named two more.

The six above are answered and gone from the console. In their place:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: unimplemented selector 33 (in 1, out 0 scalars)
    IOMobileFramebuffer: unimplemented selector 74 (in 1 scalar, out 1 scalar/64 bytes)

with backboardd back to SIGABRT, which is to say past the null pointer and
asserting again somewhere further on.

    33   kern_EnableDisableVideoPowerSavings   in 1
    74   kern_SupportedFrameInfo               in 1, out 1 + 64 bytes

33 is accepted and dropped. 74 is the first one whose answer is a structure
rather than a number, and the layout of those 64 bytes is not known here, so
none is invented: it answers none supported, with the structure zeroed, and
logs the scalar that came in. If that turns out to be the wrong answer, the
kind being asked about is the first thing worth knowing.

Both were answered, and the boot after that was the same as the first one:
SIGABRT, BKDisplay.m:417, the identical two offsets, and not one
"unimplemented selector" line on the console. Nothing new was being asked
for and the display was still not being found.

So the rest of the chain was read out of QuartzCore rather than guessed at,
and it turns out to be accounted for end to end.

    -[CAWindowServer _detectDisplays], run once from a pthread_once:
      for each of three kinds -- AppleCLCDDisplay, AppleRGBOutDisplay,
      AppleWirelessDisplay:
        count = display_count_of_type(kind)
        for i in 0..count:
          d = create_display_of_type(kind, i, block)
          if (!d->iomfb_ref) { destroy; continue; }
          s = d->new_server()
          [self addDisplay:[[CAWindowServerDisplay alloc]
                            _initWithCADisplayServer:s]]

    display_count_of_type(kind):
      list = IOMobileFramebufferCreateDisplayList()
      count the entries whose info->type == kind

and the display list comes from _iomfb_populate_display_infos, which walks
the same IOMobileFramebuffer services this file registers and decides each
one's type like this:

    if (no "AppleTV" property)                 -- a handset, so: us
            GetDisplay(&p, 0, 0)               -- "the one that is NOT main"
            type = (p && p->service == this) ? 1 : 0

That call is meant to fail on a handset: there is one framebuffer, it is the
main display, and nothing matches "not main". The type is therefore 0, the
name is "primary", and AppleCLCDDisplay is the kind that picks it up --
which also means answering selector 18 with 1 is load bearing in a second
place, since answering 0 would file this display as an external one and
leave the window server with nothing.

Everything downstream of that lines up too. The display's constructor is the
one that calls GetID, GetDisplaySize, SetDebugFlags, SetDisplayDevice,
EnableDisableVideoPowerSavings and GetColorRemapMode -- the six selectors
the console named -- so it ran; the EAGLServer constructor is the only
caller of SupportedFrameInfo, so that ran too; -initWithCADisplayServer: is
[super init] and a calloc and cannot fail; and -[CAWindowServer addDisplay:]
appends without a condition beyond rejecting a duplicate. The names agree as
well: AppleCLCDDisplay passes false for the TVOut flag, so its server is
named "LCD", and +[CADisplay mainDisplay] is precisely the display whose
name is that.

In other words there is nothing left in the static picture that can refuse
us, which is the point at which reading stops being worth anything. What is
left differs only in the values that cross the boundary and the order they
cross it in:

  - a selector answered with something the framework rejects quietly,
  - an entry point that is not externalMethod and so has never printed
    anything -- the framework calls IOConnectSetNotificationPort twice, for
    the vsync run loop source and for hot plug detect, and IOUserClient
    refuses it by default,
  - or the display being built correctly and _CASGetDisplays, the mach call
    CADisplay makes back to the window server for the list, returning
    nothing -- in which case nothing in this file is at fault and the hunt
    moves into backboardd.

Telling those apart needed the conversation rather than another guess, so
that boot printed it: every call with its selector, the scalars in, the
scalars out and the result; every client numbered, so that the opens the
framework makes while probing could be told from the one the window server
keeps and so that each call could be attributed to one or the other; and a
line from each of the entry points that used to be silent.

It came back the same on every backboardd, which crash-loops:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: [1] opened by pid 61
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [1] selector 18 in 0{}/0b out 1->1{1}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [1] closed
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] opened by pid 61
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 18 in 0{}/0b out 1->1{1}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 57 in 0{}/0b out 1->1{0}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 7  in 0{}/0b out 1->1{1}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 15 in 2{0,16}/0b out 1->1{0}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 22 in 1{0}/0b out 0->0{}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 8  in 0{}/0b out 2->2{720,1280}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 33 in 1{0}/0b out 0->0{}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 74 in 1{0}/0b out 1->1{0}/64b ret 0x0
    exit: backboardd[61] no exit reason (signal 6, status 0)
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] client died
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] closed

Client [1] is the display list asking for a display that is not the main
one, being told that this one is, and letting go -- which is the answer that
files this display as type 0, "primary", and hands it to AppleCLCDDisplay.
Client [2] is OpenByName("primary") and then the AppleDisplay constructor in
its own order: GetColorRemapMode, then update_framebuffer with GetID,
SetDebugFlags, SetDisplayDevice, GetDisplaySize and
EnableDisableVideoPowerSavings, then new_server(). Selector 74 is reached
only from the EAGLServer constructor, so the display had been built and the
server made.

And then nothing. Not one further call, and in particular no
registerNotificationPort, which is the thing to look for: the framework's
run-loop sources are set up by _kern_GetRunLoopSource, which calls
IOConnectSetNotificationPort, which this driver now logs.

The absence is the answer, because the branch that was skipped is this one:

    SupportedFrameInfo(ref) -> names
    if (!names) goto no_frame_info;              -- cbz x0, 0x18530d650
    src = IOMobileFramebufferGetRunLoopSource(ref, 3);
    if (!src) goto no_frame_info;
    add_runloop_source(this, src, true);
    IOMobileFramebufferFrameInfo(ref, callback, this, 1, 0);

and add_runloop_source is not what its name suggests:

    slot = b ? 0x118 : 0x110;
    if (!this->[slot])
            this->[slot] = x_thread_create(thread_body, this);
    while (!this->[b ? 0x198 : 0x190])
            pthread_cond_wait(...);
    CFRunLoopAddSource(rl, src, kCFRunLoopDefaultMode);

It starts the IOMFBServer's own thread and waits for it to publish its run
loop. The only other call to it is in the branch taken when the display's
flags carry 0x300, which is the external-display case; on a handset this one
is the only thing that ever starts that thread. So answering "no frame info"
left the window server holding a display whose server had never run -- and
the earlier note in this file that a count of zero was survivable, because
QuartzCore's test merely skips frame-info collection, was wrong about what
else sits inside the part being skipped.

So selector 74 now answers one field. The name is deliberately none of the
five QuartzCore looks for -- it walks the list against "Presentation_time",
"Presentation_delta", "Requested_presentation", "Performance_headroom" and
"Thermal_throttle", calls ChangeFrameInfo for each one it recognises, and
passes over the rest in silence. One unrecognised name gets the list past
the test without claiming a field this driver has no value to put in.
iomfbframeinfo=0 puts the old answer back, so the two can be compared from
the command line rather than from a second build.

That got past it, and the trace moved one link on:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: [20] selector 74 in 1{0}/0b out 1->1{1}/64b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [20] registerNotificationPort type 3 refCon 0 ret 0xe00002c7
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [20] unimplemented selector 72
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [20] getTargetAndMethodForIndex 72
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [20] selector 72 in 4{7715023460,4311005440,3,0}/0b out 0->0{}/0b ret 0xe00002c7

Type 3 is what settles which branch ran. The frame-info path asks for the
run loop source of type 3; the vsync path, which sits after the jump that
used to be taken, asks for type 5. So this is the branch that had been
skipped, now taken, and everything in it ran: GetRunLoopSource returned a
source -- _kern_GetRunLoopSource builds it from IONotificationPortCreate and
never looks at what IOConnectSetNotificationPort returned -- then
add_runloop_source started the IOMFBServer's thread, then FrameInfo asked
for notifications and was told no.

Being told no there is survivable too: QuartzCore turns a failed FrameInfo
into one cleared flag bit. But these two are now the only answers in this
conversation that are refusals, and a driver that keeps neither the port nor
the callback can never send a notification, which is the next thing that has
to work at all. So both are accepted and remembered.

Selector 72 is two framework calls wearing one number, told apart by the
first scalar:

    kern_EnableNotifications(ref, type, cb, ctx)
        { trampoline[type], ref, type, 0 }
    kern_DisableNotifications(ref, type)
        { 0,                0,   type, 0 }

The first two scalars are a function and a context in the caller's address
space; they are what goes into the mach message a real driver sends to the
port registered for that type, where IONotificationPort's dispatcher calls
the one with the other. The port arrives through registerNotificationPort,
which IOUserClient refuses by default and which this now keeps -- balanced
with releaseNotificationPort, as IOUserClient.h requires, on replacement and
in free(). Nothing is sent yet. This is the remembering, not the telling.

iomfbnotify=0 puts both refusals back.

Both were accepted, and the next boot named the next one:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: [426] selector 74 in 1{0}/0b out 1->1{1}/64b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [426] registerNotificationPort type 3 refCon 0 ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [426] notifications on for type 3
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [426] selector 72 in 4{8048945764,4383430656,3,0}/0b ret 0x0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [426] unimplemented selector 73
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [426] selector 73 in 2{3,0}/0b out 0->0{}/0b ret 0xe00002c7

73 is kern_ChangeFrameInfo(ref, mask), which sends { 3, mask }: the 3 is the
frame-info notification type, hard coded in the framework, and the mask says
which fields to collect. QuartzCore builds that mask from the names it
recognised, it recognises none of the one this driver advertises, and so the
mask is 0. There is nothing to turn on and nothing to answer with.

That is three in a row -- 74 uncovered 72, 72 uncovered 73 -- each costing a
build, a flash and a boot, and each the same kind of call: a setter, with no
scalars and no structure asked for in return. A selector like that cannot be
a question. It is the caller changing something, and the only two answers
are "done" and "this driver is broken". Refusing says the second, and that
is the wrong one, because there is no hardware here to configure and the
caller has nothing to do differently.

So the rest of that kind are answered without being named first: an unknown
selector that asks for nothing back is accepted and dropped. One that does
want something back is still refused, which is the honest answer there --
inventing a zero width or a zero surface for a caller that asked for one is
a wrong answer that travels, while a refusal stops where it happens and
prints its own number. iomfbaccept=0 goes back to refusing both.
